You are an adversarial reviewer of scientific figures for a research proposal. The attached PNGs are rendered at 2x from SVG and will be displayed about 850 px wide. Try hard to find reasons each figure should not ship. For EACH image, by file order, report: (a) overlapping or colliding text, (b) lines or arrows that cross text or other arrows confusingly, (c) arrows whose direction or endpoints are ambiguous, (d) text too small to read at display size, (e) excess text that belongs in a caption, (f) the one main point and whether a reader gets it in 5 seconds, (g) concrete fixes with coordinates or element names. Then give a verdict per figure: ship / fix / redraw. Focus: Round 2 of proposal 06, 'Who does self-improvement leave behind?' (a self-improving agent loop that keeps edits lowering mean error can harm a pre-defined minority, the StaBLE fall-risk group). All eight figures were revised after round 1. Points, in file order: (1) concept-mean-vs-tail: illustrative, under accept-if-the-mean-improves mean error falls every generation while fall-risk-group error stops gaining and climbs back past its start. (2) edit-effect-scatter: illustrative, with the tail weighted 0.125 the mean rule's acceptance line is nearly vertical, so under negative correlation many accepted edits harm the tail (orange). (3) drift-sign-map: computed from the law, the threshold correlation rho* below which selection lowers the tail depends on tail share and relative spread, not on noise; -0.143 at pi = 0.125 with equal spreads. (4) timing-regimes-geometry: schematic Fisher geometric model, the seed's position relative to the ball joining tail and mean optima gives three regimes (stays outside: gain; enters the ball: inverted U peaking at entry; starts inside: decline). (5) guarantee-validity-and-price: preliminary simulation, reusing the same tail people or testing per step against the parent breaks the guarantee, fresh people and checkpoint certification keep it, and majority gain kept rises with tail people per test. (6) instrumented-loop: every candidate is scored on guard people hidden from the loop, the drift log pairs each guard tail effect with the keep or reject decision, and a forecast frozen at generation 10 is compared with the tail change on sealed test people. (7) stable-person-splits: five-fold person-level rotation of StaBLE blocks into sealed test, guard and dev, fall-risk people stratified across blocks. (8) dose-response-design: illustrative Global-MMLU prediction, tail share sets the sign of drift (flip near pi = 0.23) while tail evaluation size changes only its magnitude. Judge each figure on its own; give ship unless a real defect remains.. Do not run commands or modify files.

